WEBVTT
TITLE Memory Management And Virtual Memory — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A program is written with logical addresses; the MMU turns them into physical ones at run time.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Contiguous allocation leaves holes. Five holes, four requests, and the fit rule decides everything.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
First-fit puts 212 in the 500 hole, 417 in the 600 hole, 112 in the 288 left by the first placement, and then stalls at 426.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
Best-fit takes the smallest hole that fits and places all four. Same requests, same holes, different outcome.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Paging cuts the address space into equal pages and memory into equal frames, so any page fits any frame.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
The page table maps a page number to a frame number. The offset is copied through unchanged.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
With 4 kilobyte pages, the low 12 bits are the offset and the remaining 20 are the page number.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Page 3 sits in frame 7, so offset 498 becomes physical address 29170.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
Page table size is entries times entry width. Nothing else enters the formula.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
GATE 2015 Set 1: 32-bit addresses, 4 kilobyte pages, 4 byte entries. One million entries, four megabytes.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
One table of a million entries must be contiguous, so the split keeps each table inside a single page.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
Two levels: 10 bits, then 10 bits, then 12 offset bits. The address is fully accounted for.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
The TLB is a small cache of recent translations, so a repeated page number skips the page table.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
A hit costs the buffer lookup plus one memory access; a miss costs the lookup plus two.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
GATE 2014 Set 3: 10 milliseconds to search, 80 to access memory, hit ratio 0.6. The answer is 122.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
The effective time falls as the hit ratio rises: 170 at zero, 122 at 0.6, 90 at one.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Demand paging loads a page only when it is touched, so a program can be larger than memory.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
Six steps: trap, save state, find the page, choose a victim, read from disk, restart.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
GATE 2011: memory takes 20 nanoseconds, a fault costs 10 milliseconds, one fault in a million accesses.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
The same formula at a fault rate of one in a thousand gives about ten thousand nanoseconds.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
When every frame is full, the replacement policy decides who leaves. Three policies, one string.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The first three references fill the frames. Reference 1 is the fourth fault and evicts page 4.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
Under least recently used the run ends at six faults, which is the paper's key for this string.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
First in first out also gives six here; optimal gives five. The gap is the price of not knowing the future.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
Belady's anomaly: with first in first out, more frames can mean more faults.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
Nine faults with three frames, ten with four. The extra frame changed the eviction order, not the demand.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Least recently used goes ten, then eight. Optimal goes seven, then six. Neither can rise.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
The stack property is the reason: with more frames the resident set only ever grows.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
GATE 2022: four frames, least recently used, fifteen references, nine faults, so the rate is 0.6.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
GATE 2010: one hundred pages forward then backward, four frames, first in first out. 196 faults.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
GATE 2025: optimal with a four-reference lookahead on twelve references gives six faults.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
Three counting shapes: a rate, a reversal, a window. Each has a shortcut that avoids the full table.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
GATE 2024 Set 2: how many pages does a two-level table occupy for 2000 pages? Between 3 and 1025.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Alternatives: an inverted table has one entry per frame, and a hashed table avoids the walk.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
Segmentation is visible to the programmer: variable-length segments, a base and a limit each.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Thrashing: the fault rate feeds back into the frame count, so more processes can mean less progress.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
Step one: write down the configuration in powers of two before computing anything.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Step two: for a time question, draw both paths and weight them by the probability.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Step three: for a replacement question, simulate the named policy and only that policy.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Five traps: unit mixing, translated offsets, one-level thinking, first in first out monotonicity, and one string.

